  abstract = {Abstract Background Bleeding remains a major complication of extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), yet early dynamic markers that precede clinically documented bleeding are poorly defined. We investigated whether the early within-patient platelet-count trajectory was associated with subsequent bleeding. Methods We conducted a retrospective cohort study using MIMIC-IV version 3.1 and MIMIC-IV-Note version 2.2. Adults with direct ICU documentation of ECMO support were eligible. An 18-hour landmark was used to exclude events occurring during exposure assessment. The association between the platelet-count log-slope during the first 18 hours and subsequent provisional bleeding was evaluated with Cox regression using 20 multiply imputed datasets. Results Among 208 adult ICU stays with direct evidence of ECMO, 169 met landmark eligibility and 72 experienced subsequent provisional bleeding within 168 hours of ECMO initiation. Platelet trajectories were estimable in 151 stays, including 64 events. A more favorable early platelet trajectory—reflecting less decline or greater recovery—was associated with a lower hazard of subsequent provisional bleeding (adjusted hazard ratio [HR] per 1-standard-deviation increase in log-slope, 0.63; 95% CI, 0.46–0.88; P = 0.007). Conclusions In this hypothesis-generating single-center study, a steeper early platelet decline preceded a higher hazard of subsequent provisional bleeding during ECMO. These findings support further evaluation of platel},
}

